From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] aic8800: wake the TX thread once per burst of frames

aicwf_frame_tx woke the SDIO TX thread for every frame. That thread runs
SCHED_FIFO, so at the first frame of a burst (the segments of a UDP GSO
send, a qdisc batch) it preempted the sender and sent what was queued so
far: a burst of 16 datagrams went out in several SDIO transfers, each with
a host claim, a flow-control register read (CMD52) and a data write
(CMD53), each waited for. Wake it only when no more frames of the burst
follow (netdev_xmit_more), and when flow control stops the queue, since
the stack then sends nothing more.

diff --git a/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_sdio.c b/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_sdio.c
--- a/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_sdio.c
+++ b/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_sdio.c
@@ -2003,7 +2003,12 @@
 
     atomic_inc(&sdiodev->tx_priv->tx_pktcnt);
     spin_unlock_bh(&sdiodev->tx_priv->txqlock);
-    complete(&bus_if->bustx_trgg);
+    /* More frames of the same burst follow (UDP GSO segments, a qdisc
+     * batch): wake the TX thread for the last one only. The thread runs
+     * SCHED_FIFO, so waking it at the first preempted this sender and sent
+     * a burst in several SDIO transfers, each with its flow-control read. */
+    if (!netdev_xmit_more())
+        complete(&bus_if->bustx_trgg);
 
 	flowctrl:
 #ifdef CONFIG_TX_NETIF_FLOWCTRL
@@ -2014,6 +2019,9 @@
 		if (!sdiodev->flowctrl) {
 			sdiodev->flowctrl = 1;
 			aicwf_sdio_tx_netif_flowctrl(sdiodev->rwnx_hw, true);
+			/* The stack sends nothing more while the queue is stopped:
+			 * the frames held back above go now. */
+			complete(&bus_if->bustx_trgg);
 		}
 	}
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&sdiodev->tx_flow_lock, flags);
